{"name":"Pyrite","hex":"#f8c642","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pyrite","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The common mineral iron disulfide (FeS2), of a pale brass-yellow color and brilliant metallic luster, crystallizing in the isometric system.","(usually as a plural: pyrites) Any metallic-looking sulphide, such as the above, which is the most common."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pyrite"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pyrite","blocks":[{"text":"The mineral pyrite ( PY-ryte), or iron pyrite, also known as fool's gold, is an iron sulfide with the chemical formula FeS2 (iron (II) disulfide). Pyrite is the most abundant sulfide mineral."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Pyrite_-_Huanzala_mine%2C_Huallanca%2C_Bolognesi%2C_Ancash%2C_Peru.jpg/330px-Pyrite_-_Huanzala_mine%2C_Huallanca%2C_Bolognesi%2C_Ancash%2C_Peru.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pyrite","wikidata":"Q50769"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/2780M-pyrite1.jpg/500px-2780M-pyrite1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/2780M-pyrite1.jpg/1280px-2780M-pyrite1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/2780M-pyrite1.jpg/500px-2780M-pyrite1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pyrite cubic crystals on marl from Navajún, La Rioja, Spain (size: 95 by 78 millimetres [3.7 by 3.1 in], 512 grams [18.1 oz]; main crystal: 31 millimetres [1.2 in] on edge)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2780M-pyrite1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/14/Pyrite_under_Normal_and_Polarized_light.jpg/500px-Pyrite_under_Normal_and_Polarized_light.jpg?